Joe Pollock (pollock@u.washington.edu) wrote:

> What about the situation where game playing _does_ interfere with
> academic usage?  I'm speaking of the situation where resources are
> limited (modems, server ports, user limits, etc.) with no immediate
> prospects for relief.  Sure, if we get the grants we've applied for...
> but that's about a year off.

	I think your best bet is to set a policy about when or if users
	can access facilities for reasons that aren't related to
	instruction or research. Why do you have compute facilties? Ours
	are here with one stated purpose - support the instructional and
	research program of the College. Same sort of purpose as the
	College Library.

	You also have to explain this point to persons who might be
	causing problems. I've done it with lots of students. They have
	a very hard time justifying recreational use of a facility that
	is funded only for academic purposes.

	After you set a policy and publish and post it, you have to
	enforce it. in our case we remove login privileges. THey could
	be restored if a student gets a faculty member to request it.

	This has worked fairly well to cut down on games and gamers
	interfering with legitamate activity. You won;t be able to stop
	it. You ought to be able to discourage it enough so students can
	police the problem on their own.

	Our facilities are to be used only for academic purposes from
	8AM to 11PM Monday-Friday. 
	academic

> In our PC labs, academic users have immediate bumping rights.  But on
> a multiuser system, there may be no ready way to identify gamers, a 
> gamer probably won't notice how busy the system has become (assuming
